NAME
       groups - print the groups a user belongs to

SYNOPSIS
       groups [USER]...

DESCRIPTION
       groups prints the names of the groups a user is a member of: first the
       primary group, then the supplementary groups. With no USER it shows the
       groups of the current shell. With one or more USER names it prints each
       name, a colon and that user's groups, as read from /etc/passwd and
       /etc/group.

       Group changes (usermod -aG) do not reach a shell that is already running.
       "groups student" reads the files and shows the new list at once, while a
       plain "groups" in student's old shell still shows the old list until
       student logs in again (or runs newgrp).

EXAMPLES
       Show your own groups:
              $ groups
              student wheel

       Check that a new membership was written:
              # usermod -aG wheel student
              # groups student
              student : student wheel

SEE ALSO
       id(1), usermod(8), group(5), newgrp(1)
